      "executive_summary": "This is the first scored cycle on this repository: the strict score moved from 24.2 to 78.8 purely because twenty blind reviews replaced an unreviewed subjective baseline of zero. No work has been executed against the plan yet, so there is no rework history, no score churn and no wontfix debt to mine. Treating the 78.8 as an achievement would be a mistake; it is a measurement, and the 66 open items are the actual starting position.\n\nThe decisive strategic fact is that the reviews did not come back with a uniform wash of style complaints. Three of them reproduced live functional defects by running the code: txBatteryStatus is published as a bitmask (0 or 2**i) while the YAML binary sensors match payload_on: 1, so channels 2 through 7 can never report a low battery; Controller.__init__ returns early on a bad configuration and leaves mqtt_client unset, so shutDown raises AttributeError; and a None txBatteryStatus raises TypeError inside the preprocessor, which drops the entire packet. A fourth, confirmed independently during triage preparation, is that set_language and set_config_overrides are silently inert once any lazy cache in utils has been populated. These are defects of the same class and severity as the four this branch already fixes, and they must be sequenced ahead of every elegance item.\n\nThe second decisive fact is a hard scope boundary. This work lands on a branch whose open pull request is a bugfix PR carrying four defects with their own regression tests. The split rule for this cycle is therefore: behaviour-correcting fixes and local cleanups inside already-touched files stay in that PR; structural refactors (the package import cycle, splitting controller.py's archive aggregation, decomposing utils.py) move to a follow-up PR. Six reviewers converged on the import topology and four on the controller/utils overload, which makes those the highest-value items in the backlog and simultaneously the ones this cycle must not touch. Recording them as deferred with an explicit pointer to the follow-up PR is the correct outcome, not a failure to execute.",
      "observe_guidance": "Verification effort is best spent where reviewers made falsifiable claims and least well spent where they expressed taste. Re-run the reproductions: the battery bitmap against the YAML payload_on values, the shutDown AttributeError after a rejected config, the None txBatteryStatus path, and the locale setter inertness. At least one high-confidence finding is already known to be wrong -- api_surface_coherence claims get_key_config returns None for unknown keys, when it has a three-step fallback that always returns a dict and therefore cannot produce the TypeError the finding predicts -- so treat every stated confidence level as a hypothesis and check the code or run it. Expect heavy duplication: the import cycle appears under six dimension names and the controller/utils overload under four; collapse those into one item each rather than carrying near-identical entries. Watch for findings that merely restate a deliberate design decision, such as the 'unused config_publisher parameter' on the conversion lambdas, which is a uniform signature for a registry of callables.",
      "reflect_guidance": "Focus on test_strategy, the two elegance dimensions, contract_coherence and logic_clarity, in that order of headroom but with confirmed defects first regardless of dimension. Avoid the package import topology and any split of controller.py or utils.py: they are the strongest findings in the set and belong to the follow-up PR, so the right move is to defer them cleanly rather than part-fix them. There are no recurring loops to guard against in a first cycle; the equivalent risk here is the opposite one, of over-fixing into a bugfix PR and making it unreviewable. Also reconcile the one genuine contradiction in the set: reviewers variously recommend removing the dead 'unit_system is not None' guards and hardening the same call paths against None, and only one of those can be right.",
      "organize_guidance": "Order the queue as: (1) confirmed functional defects with reproductions, each with a regression test that fails against the current code; (2) contract fixes that make a documented promise true (the no-op claim in _augment_db_derived, the locale setters, the half-initialised Controller); (3) test-suite work, starting with the reset_locale_state fixture that six modules trust and that cannot do its job; (4) local cleanups in files the branch already touches -- dead guards, legacy typing in models, boilerplate docstrings and entry logs, the missing @classmethod; (5) everything structural, deferred with a note naming the follow-up PR. Do not churn controller.py or utils.py beyond what tiers 1 to 4 require, because the follow-up PR will rewrite both and any cosmetic edit made here becomes a merge conflict there.",
      "sense_check_guidance": "The value trap in this cycle is score-chasing through volume: twenty or thirty docstring and typing edits would move the mechanical needle while leaving a weather station that cannot report five of its eight low-battery channels. Judge the plan by whether the reproduced defects are fixed and covered by tests that fail without the fix, not by item count. The second trap is a plan that quietly swallows the deferred structural work; verify that each deferred item carries a note stating where it goes, so the follow-up PR can be opened from the plan alone. Finally, check that no item proposes editing a file this branch does not already touch for cosmetic reasons.",
      "anti_patterns": [],
