uster \"timestamp-lambda-naming\"\n- review::.::holistic::type_safety::bare_type_ignore -> cluster \"mypy-weewx-import-overrides\"\n- review::.::holistic::convention_outlier::mixed_typing_idioms -> cluster \"models-typing-and-validator\"\n- review::.::holistic::type_safety::legacy_typing_half_migrated -> cluster \"models-typing-and-validator\"\n- review::.::holistic::convention_outlier::stationinfo_validator_missing_classmethod -> cluster \"models-typing-and-validator\"\n- review::.::holistic::dependency_health::unused_runtime_setuptools -> cluster \"dependency-and-readme-hygiene\"\n- review::.::holistic::dependency_health::weewx_unpinned_runtime -> cluster \"dependency-and-readme-hygiene\"\n- review::.::holistic::incomplete_migration::vestigial_time_zone_shim -> cluster \"dependency-and-readme-hygiene\"\n- review::.::holistic::ai_generated_debt::boilerplate_docstrings_and_entry_logs -> cluster \"docstring-and-entry-log-trim\"\n- review::.::holistic::test_strategy::controller_event_paths_untested -> cluster \"coverage-gaps-controller-and-catalogue\"\n- review::.::holistic::test_strategy::no_cross_module_integration_test -> cluster \"coverage-gaps-controller-and-catalogue\"\n- review::.::holistic::api_surface_coherence::get_key_config_returns_none -> cluster \"coverage-gaps-controller-and-catalogue\"\n- review::.::holistic::cross_module_architecture::submodules_import_via_package_init -> skip \"defer-to-followup-pr/import-topology canonical entry - break the seven-file package-root cycle and re-enable isort there, not in a four-defect bugfix PR\"\n- review::.::holistic::high_level_elegance::package_self_import_and_order_dependence -> skip \"defer-to-followup-pr/import-topology duplicate of the canonical cycle entry - same seven files, no extra content\"\n- review::.::holistic::dependency_health::package_root_import_cycle -> skip \"defer-to-followup-pr/import-topology duplicate - adds only the re-enable-isort clause\"\n- review::.::holistic::design_coherence::package_facade_import_cycle -> skip \"defer-to-followup-pr/import-topology duplicate - its seven-file count matches the detector cycle item\"\n- review::.::holistic::initialization_coupling::circular_import_order_dependency -> skip \"defer-to-followup-pr/import-topology duplicate - the hand-ordered import list is the same artefact\"\n- review::.::holistic::package_organization::models_imports_package_root -> skip \"defer-to-followup-pr/import-topology stage 1 - the single safe edit that breaks the models-to-root edge, named as the first step of the follow-up branch\"\n- review::.::holistic::convention_outlier::mixed_intra_package_import_style -> skip \"defer-to-followup-pr/import-topology convention facet - config_publisher.py is the existing precedent for the target style\"\n- review::.::holistic::high_level_elegance::db_aggregation_in_controller -> skip \"defer-to-followup-pr/module-decomposition canonical entry - extract the archive-DB aggregation out of the WeeWX service class\"\n- review::.::holistic::mid_level_elegance::db_aggregates_in_controller -> skip \"defer-to-followup-pr/module-decomposition duplicate - adds the import-time obs_group_dict mutation that moves with the aggregator\"\n- review::.::holistic::design_coherence::controller_db_aggregation_mixed -> skip \"defer-to-followup-pr/module-decomposition duplicate - its in-scope table-driven half is owned by archive-derived-atomic-update\"\n- review::.::holistic::high_level_elegance::utils_catch_all_module -> skip \"defer-to-followup-pr/module-decomposition canonical entry for splitting utils.py into units and sensor-catalogue modules\"\n- review::.::holistic::abstraction_fitness::generic_utils_module -> skip \"defer-to-followup-pr/module-decomposition duplicate of the utils split\"\n- review::.::holistic::mid_level_elegance::duplicate_unit_conversion_seam -> skip \"defer-to-followup-pr/module-decomposition - redundant work only, to_std_system verified idempotent and non-mutating, so converting once means redrawing the publisher boundary, which the follow-up PR does anyway\"\n- review::.::holistic::naming_quality::process_packet_generic_verb -> skip \"defer-to-followup-pr/api-naming canonical entry - renaming three same-named public methods changes the published surface across four modules and the tests\"\n- review::.::holistic::api_surface_coherence::process_packet_divergent_contracts -> skip \"defer-to-followup-pr/api-naming duplicate - same three methods, same three return contracts\"\n- review::.::holistic::test_strategy::controller_tests_use_new_stubs -> skip \"defer-to-followup-pr/module-decomposition - the clean fix is the archive extraction, which hands these tests a public surface for free instead of a Controller factory fixture invented now\"\n- review::.::holistic::abstraction_fitness::convert_lambda_unused_param -> skip \"yagni - the unused second parameter is a deliberate uniform signature for a name-addressed converter registry referenced from sensors.yaml, so changing it edits five lambdas, two call sites and a published contract to delete one identifier\"\n- review::.::holistic::naming_quality::key_config_unit_metadata_near_duplicate_names -> skip \"yagni - both names are re-exported in the package __all__, so the rename is a public API break for a taste preference with no reader actually blocked\"\n- review::.::holistic::mid_level_elegance::publisher_shared_mutable_state -> skip \"not-worth-it - the plain-dict choice, the build-then-insert ordering and the list() snapshots are each already deliberate and commented, so a lock-guarded accessor replaces a working documented design with a heavier one for no observed defect\"\n- review::.::holistic::contract_coherence::key_config_accessor_inconsistent_copy_semantics -> skip \"not-worth-it - no caller mutates the shared mapping and a MappingProxyType would break the existing list(...).items() snapshot idiom, so only its one-line import half is worth acting on and that is owned by dead-code-and-residue-deletion\"\n- review::.::holistic::type_safety::sensor_config_untyped_dict -> skip \"over-engineering - the key config is deep-merged from arbitrary user YAML and carries arbitrary Home Assistant discovery keys, so any TypedDict permissive enough to accept it is information-free, and the proposed dict[str, float | None] packet type is wrong because packets carry str and int values\"\n- review::.::holistic::api_surface_coherence::publisher_ctor_wide_positional -> skip \"exaggerated - the Optional-versus-required unit_system mismatch it asks to reconcile does not exist, both constructors declare the same non-Optional default, and its surviving dead-branch half is owned by dead-code-and-residue-deletion while keyword-only constructor args are churn inside a bugfix PR\"\n\n## Strategy \u2014 cluster blueprint and PR split for the 66 open review issues\n\nObserve settled what is true. This stage decides what we do, what we deliberately do not do,\nand \u2014 the decision that actually shapes this cycle \u2014 which side of a PR boundary each item\nlands on. The branch `claude/home-assistant-integration-e7acd4` already carries an open bugfix\nPR with four fixed defects and their regression tests. Everything below is sorted by that\nboundary first and by dimension headroom second.\n\nOf 66 open issues, 53 are genuine but only 11 are distinct problems once duplication collapses.\nThat ratio is the whole story: 25 of the 53 genuine entries are the same five problems seen from\ndifferent dimensions. So the blueprint has 13 clusters, not 53, and the duplicates are folded\ninto a canonical cluster rather than carried as separate work.\n\n## The split rule, applied\n\n- **Stays in PR #1 (13 clusters, 44 issues):** the four reproduced defects, the contract fixes\n  that make an existing documented promise true, the test work those fixes require, and local\n  cleanups that are provably dead code or are confined to a file the fixes already open.\n- **Deferred to the follow-up PR (16 issues, 4 named groups):** every structural refactor \u2014\n  the package-root import cycle, the archive-DB extraction out of controller.py, the utils.py\n  decomposition, and the cross-module public-method rename. These are the strongest findings in\n  the set, which is exactly why they must not land next to four unrelated bug fixes. Each\n  deferred ledger line names its destination group so the follow-up PR can be opened from plan\n  state alone.\n- **Skipped permanently (6 issues):** four YAGNI/not-worth-it judgement calls I agree with, one\n  over-engineering rejection, one whose central claim observe disproved.\n\nThe one cosmetic cluster that reaches a file the branch does not already touch is\n`models-typing-and-validator`. It stays in PR #1 because the split rule names it explicitly, but\nit is sequenced last so the follow-up branch can be cut before it lands: its edits sit in the\nsame import block that `defer-to-followup-pr/import-topology` stage 1 rewrites, and that is the\nonly merge-conflict surface between the two PRs in the entire plan.\n\n## Dimension reading\n\nNo dimension is recurring in the loop sense \u2014 this is the first scored cycle, so every dimension\nhas open issues and none has resolved ones, and `detect_recurring_patterns` therefore has nothing\nto report. The equivalent signal here is cross-dimension convergence, and it is strong:\n\n- `logic_clarity` and `contract_coherence` are the only dimensions that produced reproduced,\n  user-visible defects, and they are the two that own the front of the queue.\n- `high_level_elegance`, `mid_level_elegance`, `design_coherence`, `cross_module_architecture`,\n  `dependency_health`, `package_organization`, `initialization_coupling` and\n  `abstraction_fitness` converged on exactly two problems: the package-root import cycle and the\n  controller/utils overload. Thirteen entries, two problems, both deferred. Treating that\n  convergence as thirteen pieces of work would be the single biggest planning error available.\n- `test_strategy` has the largest headroom (42) and the lowest score, and its four entries split\n  cleanly: two are coverage gaps that belong in this cycle, one cannot be fixed without the\n  locale reset hook and so travels with it, and one is only fixable by the deferred extraction.\n- `low_level_elegance`, `type_safety`, `ai_generated_debt` and `convention_outlier` are where the\n  score-chasing trap lives. They contribute the dead-guard deletions (real, provable, cheap) and\n  the docstring trim (narrowed to two docstrings and four log lines, sequenced last).\n\n## Cluster Blueprint\n\nExecution order is the numbering. Dependencies are stated explicitly.\n\n**1. `preprocessor-battery-bitmap`** \u2014 src/weewx_ha/preprocessor.py, new tests/\nThe headline defect plus everything else in the same 15-line file. Publish `(bitmap >> i) & 1`\ninstead of `bitmap & (1 << i)` so all eight fields are 0/1 against `payload_on: 1`; guard a None\nbitmap before the loop while still deleting the key; rename the camelCase local; delete the\nno-op `__init__` and the two content-free entry logs. Scope correction the item text must carry:\nchannels 1 through 7 are broken, 7 of 8 fields, not channels 2-7. Regression test asserts 0/1 for\nbitmaps 0, 1 and 255 and a None bitmap that does not raise, and must be shown failing first.\nNo dependencies. This file is at 32% coverage with lines 29-37 at zero, which is why it shipped.\n\n**2. `controller-init-lifecycle-contract`** \u2014 src/weewx_ha/controller.py, models/extension_config.py, tests/\nOne problem reported five times plus two adjacent fixes in the same 60 lines. On a rejected\nconfig, `__init__` returns at :109 leaving all seven attributes unset and `shutDown` raises\nAttributeError at :480. Fix: set an explicit disabled flag and initialise the attributes, make\n`shutDown` a no-op when there is no client. Fold in the ordering fix \u2014 construct both publishers\nbefore `init_mqtt_client`, which calls `loop_start()` at :198 \u2014 because that is what lets the two\n`getattr(self, \"config_publisher\", None)` guards at :216 and :244 be deleted rather than kept.\nFold in the error-context tidy: the ValidationError is currently logged twice (model then\ncontroller), `raise e` should be a bare `raise`, and :487 is the one handler missing `exc_info`.\nNo dependencies. Owns the shutDown-after-bad-config regression test.\n\n**3. `locale-cache-invalidation`** \u2014 src/weewx_ha/locale_loader.py, utils.py, tests/conftest.py and six test modules\nOne problem reported five times, plus the test fixture that is its victim. `set_language` and\n`set_config_overrides` assign module globals while utils holds four caches that nothing ever\ninvalidates, so both setters are silently inert after first use. Fix: a single\n`utils.configure(language, overrides)` that sets state and clears all four caches, called from\nthe controller. Then, and only then, move the `reset_locale_state` fixture into conftest.py as\none autouse fixture. Ordering inside the cluster is load-bearing: centralising the fixture first\nwould centralise a false guarantee, since it currently cannot reach `_KEY_CONFIG`,\n`_SENSORS_YAML`, `_UNIT_METADATA` or `_ENUM_MAPS` at all. Explicitly rejected here: the\n`functools.cache` suggestion, which would hide the caches behind a decorator and make the\ninertness harder to see, and the full injected-catalogue redesign, which is follow-up work.\nNo dependencies.\n\n**4. `archive-derived-atomic-update`** \u2014 src/weewx_ha/controller.py:346-414\n`_augment_db_derived` documents \"No-op on failure (logged)\" but writes into the caller's dict\nincrementally inside one try, so a mid-way failure publishes a partial set. Accumulate into a\nlocal dict and `update()` as the last statement in the try \u2014 four lines, and it makes the\nexisting docstring true rather than editing the docstring to match the bug. While in the\nfunction, table-drive the three near-identical day-extreme blocks as\n(column, descending, value_key, time_key) and separate the rain/ET sums from the extremes.\nTwo clauses struck as stale: \"use HOUR_S\" is already done (:80-81, used at :385-386) and the\nremaining `/ 3600.0` at :383 is a seconds-to-hours conversion, not a time window, so replacing it\nwould be wrong. The SQL-helper extraction is deferred. Depends on nothing, but must land before\nthe follow-up extraction so the contract fix is not rewritten twice.\n\n**5. `controller-callback-typing-and-submit`** \u2014 src/weewx_ha/controller.py:202-342\nAdd a `_submit(fn, *args, callback=check_future_errors)` helper: the submit plus\nadd_done_callback pair occurs 7 times, not the 5 the finding claims. Rename the two callbacks\nwhose names understate what they do (one submits discovery, the other fans out two tasks).\nAnnotate the eight unannotated callbacks, which is the highest-value typing work in the set\nbecause mypy currently reports two function bodies as unchecked and those are precisely the\nbodies the queue is editing. Depends on cluster 2, which rewrites the same `__init__` and\nremoves the getattr guards these call sites carry.\n\n**6. `dead-code-and-residue-deletion`** \u2014 config_publisher.py, state_publisher.py, utils.py, controller.py\nThis cluster settles the plan's one genuine contradiction, and the verdict is DELETE, not harden.\nFour independent proofs that `unit_system` can never be None: the field is non-Optional with a\nMETRICWX default, pydantic rejects None outright, both constructors declare the same non-Optional\ndefault, and the only two call sites pass `self.config.unit_system` \u2014 plus coverage marks the\nFalse arm of config_publisher.py:119 as a never-taken branch. Delete both guards. Delete the\nthree `RuntimeError` cache guards (utils.py lines 40, 48, 56 never execute, because load_yaml\nalways returns a dict). Delete the credential guard, and strip its security framing: username is\nmin_length=1, password is a required non-empty Secret, and `bool(Secret(\"\"))` is True anyway, so\nthere is no anonymous-connect path to create. Delete the stale function-level import at\nconfig_publisher.py:160, whose module-level twin is at :14 and which guards against a cycle that\ndoes not exist on that edge. Any executor proposal to harden these paths against None must be\nrefused with this evidence. No dependencies; run it before cluster 7.\n\n**7. `locale-loader-error-contract`** \u2014 src/weewx_ha/locale_loader.py\nThe packaged base YAML ships inside the wheel, so a missing or malformed base file is a packaging\nfailure, not a runtime condition; swallowing it silently degrades every sensor to guessed\nmetadata. Raise for the base file with `from e`, keep the fallback only for the optional\nlocalized overlay. This is the one item in PR #1 that changes a startup failure mode, so it needs\nits own two tests \u2014 missing base raises, missing overlay still falls back. Depends on cluster 6,\nwhich removes the dead guards that currently paper over this.\n\n**8. `timestamp-lambda-naming`** \u2014 src/weewx_ha/utils.py:217-222, locales/sensors.yaml\n`localtime_to_utc_timestamp` is a verified mathematical identity on an epoch input, and\n`localtime_to_iso` emits UTC despite its name. Reduce the first to a passthrough or drop the\nregistry entry, rename the second to `epoch_to_iso_utc`, and update the `convert_lambda`\nreferences in the YAML catalogue. The `check_config_update` naming half of the second entry is\nalready handled by cluster 5. The arity change is rejected \u2014 see the skip decisions.\nNo dependencies.\n\n**9. `mypy-weewx-import-overrides`** \u2014 pyproject.toml plus five source files\nAdd `[[tool.mypy.overrides]] module = [\"weewx.*\", \"weeutil.*\"]` with ignore_missing_imports and\ndelete the eight inline comments. One edit that removes the only mypy error in the repo\n(state_publisher.py:8, the one weewx import left unignored) and the seven-bare-versus-one-coded\ninconsistency at the same time. Preferred over sprinkling `[import-untyped]` eight times.\nNo dependencies.\n\n**10. `coverage-gaps-controller-and-catalogue`** \u2014 tests/\ncontroller.py sits at 54% with `__init__`, all six MQTT callbacks, the three future callbacks,\nboth WeeWX event handlers and `shutDown` unexecuted; `from_config_dict` is uncovered; and\nutils.py:150-190, the whole key-name guessing fallback, is at zero. Three steps: cover\n`from_config_dict`, add exactly one cross-module integration test (fake engine plus mocked paho,\nfire both WeeWX events, assert discovery and state topics \u2014 the cheapest check that would have\ncaught the battery bug end to end), and add an unknown-key test for the catalogue fallback.\nOn that last one, record loudly that the stated defect is REJECTED: `get_key_config` has a\nthree-step fallback and always returns a dict, so the predicted TypeError at\nconfig_publisher.py:125 cannot occur and must not be \"fixed\". Only the coverage half survives.\nDepends on clusters 1, 2 and 3, whose own regression tests come first.\n\n**11. `dependency-and-readme-hygiene`** \u2014 pyproject.toml, requirements.txt, README.md\nDrop setuptools from `[project].dependencies` and wheel from requirements.txt (neither is\nimported; version resolution is build-time only). Document the supported WeeWX range in the\nREADME, and explicitly reject adding weewx to `[project].dependencies` \u2014 the extension is\ninstalled by `weectl extension install` into an existing WeeWX environment, so a pip runtime\ndependency could pull a second copy. Mark `timezone` in README.md:150 as accepted-but-ignored,\nand do NOT drop `StationInfo.time_zone`: HEAD deliberately keeps the field so existing user\nconfigs still validate, which is why the three tests that pass it are fine as they are.\nNo dependencies.\n\n**12. `models-typing-and-validator`** \u2014 models/extension_config.py, mqtt_config.py, tls_config.py, station_info.py\nConvert the three legacy-typing model files to `X | None` and builtin generics (requires-python\nis >=3.10, and tls_config.py is already half-migrated inside one file), and add the missing\n`@classmethod` plus annotations to the `time_zone` validator so it matches both sibling models.\nPydantic v2 handles the current form fine, so this is pure consistency, not a latent defect, and\nit must not outrank anything functional. Sequenced last among the code clusters for the\nconflict reason given above.\n\n**13. `docstring-and-entry-log-trim`** \u2014 config_publisher.py, state_publisher.py\nNarrowed hard: two docstrings with empty Parameters/Returns blocks and four entry-only debug\nlogs, of which two are already deleted by cluster 1. Explicit caveat for the executor: do not\ntouch the rationale comments \u2014 the thread-safety, build-then-insert and snapshot explanations are\nthe most valuable prose in the package. This is the volume-over-value item the sense-check\nguidance warns about, so it is last and it is small.\n\n## Follow-up PR manifest\n\nFour groups, each named in its ledger lines, in the order the follow-up branch should take them:\n\n1. `defer-to-followup-pr/import-topology` (7 issues) \u2014 start with stage 1, the single-line change\n   of `from .. import UnitSystem` to `from ..utils import UnitSystem` in models/extension_config.py,\n   which breaks the models-to-root edge on its own and cannot create a new cycle because utils does\n   not import models. Then convert state_publisher.py and controller.py to direct sibling imports,\n   using config_publisher.py as the in-repo precedent, and re-enable isort in both `__init__` files.\n2. `defer-to-followup-pr/module-decomposition` (6 issues) \u2014 extract the archive-DB aggregation\n   and its two SQL helpers out of the WeeWX service class, moving the import-time\n   `obs_group_dict` registration with it; split utils.py into a units module and a\n   sensor-catalogue module; and while the publisher boundary is open, convert the packet once\n   instead of twice.\n3. `defer-to-followup-pr/api-naming` (2 issues) \u2014 rename the three same-named `process_packet`\n   methods to say what each does, once the modules they live in have stopped moving.\n4. The deferred test-harness item travels with group 2, because extracting the aggregator is what\n   gives those tests a public surface instead of `Controller.__new__` and a re-attached\n   SimpleNamespace.\n\n## Backlog Decisions\n\n- auto/initial-review -> skip spent cluster, the twenty blind reviews it schedules have already run and produced the 66 review issues this ledger disposes of\n- auto/dict_keys-phantom_read -> skip both keys are written in sensors.yaml and loaded into the key catalogue, 100 percent false positive rate per observe sampling of 2 of 2\n- auto/cycles+facade -> supersede absorbed into defer-to-followup-pr/import-topology, same seven files and same root cause as the canonical review entry\n- auto/global_mutable_config -> supersede absorbed into cluster locale-cache-invalidation, the reset hook resolves both globals\n- auto/smells-genuine -> promote folded into clusters preprocessor-battery-bitmap and controller-callback-typing-and-submit, zero false positives in 4 of 4 sampled and two of the four counts are understated in the detector favour\n- auto/smells-false-positive -> skip the hardcoded URL is a required Home Assistant origin field, the magic numbers are named constants with comments, and the one TODO is an intentional roadmap note, 67 percent false positive rate per observe sampling\n- auto/smells-structural -> supersede broken up as observe recommended, the large-file item goes to defer-to-followup-pr/module-decomposition, the deferred import is promoted into dead-code-and-residue-deletion, and the two high-complexity functions are left alone because their complexity encodes documented enum-reconciliation and three-layer-priority behaviour a refactor would obscure\n\n## Skip Decisions\n\nSix permanent skips, each with a reason specific to that issue rather than a category, recorded\non its ledger line above. Four are judgement calls where I agree with observe that the fix costs\nmore than the problem: the uniform converter-registry signature, the public-API rename for near\nduplicate names, the lock-guarded publisher accessor over a design that is already deliberate and\ncommented, and the MappingProxyType wrapper that would break a working snapshot idiom. One is an\nover-engineering rejection recorded with its reason \u2014 the key config is deep-merged from\narbitrary user YAML, so no useful TypedDict exists \u2014 so that it is not re-proposed next cycle.\nThe last is the one entry whose central claim observe disproved: there is no Optional-versus-\nrequired `unit_system` mismatch to reconcile, and its only surviving half is already owned by\n`dead-code-and-residue-deletion`.\n\nSelf-check: 66 open review issues, 66 ledger lines, each issue ID appearing exactly once, split\n44 clustered / 16 deferred with a named destination / 6 permanently skipped. No issue ID is\nmentioned anywhere outside the Coverage Ledger section.\n\n",
