          "confirmed_at": "2026-11-10T11:39:21+00:00",
          "confirmed_text": "I reviewed the reflect stage against the persisted ledger: 66 cited IDs, no missing and no duplicate entries, and 66 ledger rows splitting 44 cluster / 22 permanent_skip, which matches the reported 44 in PR #1, 16 deferred to the follow-up PR and 6 genuinely skipped. The split honours the rule I set for this cycle -- behaviour-correcting fixes and local cleanups in already-touched files stay in the bugfix PR, every structural refactor (import topology, module decomposition, api naming) is deferred with its destination named so the follow-up PR can be opened from plan state. The four reproduced defects in logic_clarity, contract_coherence and initialization_coupling lead the order, and the settled questions (dead unit_system guards deleted, to_std_system safe, get_key_config claim rejected, time_zone field kept) are carried as decisions rather than left open. One reservation I will act on at organize: the locale-loader-error-contract cluster turns a tolerated missing packaged YAML into a hard startup failure, which is a risk change rather than a defect fix, so it does not belong in this PR on the rule as written.",
          "orchestrator_overrides": [
            {
              "stage_applied": "organize",
              "issue_ids": [
                "review::.::holistic::contract_coherence::loader_error_contract_conflict",
                "review::.::holistic::error_consistency::locale_load_failures_swallowed_dead_checks"
              ],
              "from": "cluster \"locale-loader-error-contract\" (PR #1)",
              "to": "permanent_skip \"defer-to-followup-pr/error-contract\" (fifth deferred group)",
              "reason": "defer-to-followup-pr/error-contract - ORCHESTRATOR OVERRIDE of reflect's routing into PR #1. The proposed fix makes a missing or malformed packaged base YAML raise instead of degrading to {}, which turns a condition a WeeWX service currently tolerates into a hard startup failure of the whole weather station. That is a risk change, not a defect fix, and this cycle's split rule keeps only behaviour-correcting fixes in the bugfix PR. It needs its own PR where the new failure mode is the headline rather than a footnote under four unrelated bug fixes. The finding is NOT dropped: the contradiction it names is real - locale_loader.load_yaml returns {} on FileNotFoundError and yaml.YAMLError while the utils getters at lines 39-40, 47-48 and 55-56 advertise a RuntimeError that can therefore never fire - and the follow-up PR must settle it in one direction (raise for the packaged base, or delete the dead RuntimeError guards and document degradation as intended)."
            }
          ]
        },
        "organize": {
          "stage": "organize",
          "report": "# ORGANIZE \u2014 12 clusters, 42 issues, 30 steps, 24 permanent skips\n\nReflect's blueprint was executed as written with one orchestrator-directed change, recorded\nbelow. Every issue ID used here was generated from `.desloppify/plan.json`'s reflect\n`disposition_ledger`, never retyped, so all 66 dispositions are accounted for exactly once:\n42 clustered, 18 deferred to a follow-up PR, 6 permanently skipped on judgement.\n\n## The one divergence from reflect\n\n`locale-loader-error-contract` (2 issues: `load_yaml` swallowing a missing or invalid packaged\nbase file while the utils getters advertise a `RuntimeError` that can never fire) was moved out\nof PR #1 into a fifth deferred group, `defer-to-followup-pr/error-contract`.\n\nReason: the proposed fix makes a missing or malformed packaged base YAML raise instead of\ndegrading to `{}`. For a WeeWX service that converts a tolerated condition into a hard startup\nfailure of the whole weather station \u2014 a risk change, not a defect fix, and this cycle's split\nrule keeps only behaviour-correcting fixes in the bugfix PR. It deserves its own PR where the new\nfailure mode is the headline rather than a footnote under four unrelated bug fixes. The finding is\nnot dropped: the contradiction it names is real, and the follow-up PR must settle it in one\ndirection. The coupling is recorded inside `dead-code-and-residue-deletion` step 2, which deletes\nthe three dead `RuntimeError` guards (utils.py:39-40, :47-48, :55-56) and explicitly instructs the\nexecutor not to touch `load_yaml` in this PR.\n\nMechanics of that change: the reflect stage is confirmed and must not be re-run, and no CLI\ncommand amends a ledger row, so the two rows were amended in place in\n`epic_triage_meta.triage_stages.reflect.disposition_ledger` and in\n`epic_triage_meta.issue_dispositions`, each carrying `original_reflect_decision` plus an\n`overridden_by` / `decision_source` marker, and the override is also logged under\n`triage_stages.reflect.orchestrator_overrides`. Nothing else in reflect's record was touched.\n\n## Priority ordering, and why\n\nReproduced defects first, each with a regression test the cluster requires to be shown failing\nagainst current code before the fix lands:\n\n1. `preprocessor-battery-bitmap` (5 issues, 2 steps) \u2014 the headline. `packet[field] =\n   bitmap & (1 << i)` publishes 0/2/4/.../128 while the catalogue matches `payload_on: 1`, so\n   7 of 8 battery sensors can never report low; only `batteryStatusISS` on bit 0 works because\n   `1 << 0 == 1`. The item text's \"channels 2-7\" is corrected to channels 1 through 7 in the step\n   detail. preprocessor.py:29-37 is at 0% coverage, which is why it shipped. The None-bitmask\n   TypeError and the file's no-op scaffolding are in the same 37-line file, so they ride along.\n2. `controller-init-lifecycle-contract` (7 issues, 4 steps) \u2014 one problem reported five times:\n   `__init__` returns at controller.py:109 leaving all seven attributes unset, and `shutDown`\n   dereferences `self.mqtt_client` at :480. Folds in the publisher-ordering fix, because\n   constructing both publishers before `init_mqtt_client` (which reaches `loop_start()` at :198)\n   is what permits deleting the `getattr(self, \"config_publisher\", None)` probes at :216 and :244\n   rather than keeping them, and the error-context tidy (`raise e` -> bare `raise` at\n   extension_config.py:66, the duplicate ValidationError log, the missing `exc_info` at :487).\n3. `locale-cache-invalidation` (6 issues, 3 steps) \u2014 `set_language` and `set_config_overrides`\n   assign module globals while utils holds four caches nothing invalidates, so both are inert\n   after first use. One `utils.configure()` that sets state and clears `_ENUM_MAPS`,\n   `_UNIT_METADATA`, `_SENSORS_YAML` and `_KEY_CONFIG`. Intra-cluster ordering is load-bearing and\n   stated in step 3: the `reset_locale_state` fixture moves into conftest.py only after the hook\n   lands, because centralising it first would centralise a false guarantee.\n\nThen the contract fix, then the mechanical work in already-open files, then tests, then the two\ncosmetic clusters:\n\n4. `archive-derived-atomic-update` (2, 2) \u2014 `_augment_db_derived` documents \"No-op on failure\"\n   but writes incrementally; accumulate and `update()` last, which makes the existing docstring\n   true instead of editing it to match the bug.\n5. `controller-callback-typing-and-submit` (3, 3) \u2014 the submit+add_done_callback pair occurs 7\n   times, not 5.\n6. `dead-code-and-residue-deletion` (6, 3) \u2014 settles the plan's one genuine contradiction, and\n   the verdict recorded in the step detail is DELETE, not harden, with the four proofs attached so\n   an executor proposing to harden against None can be refused from plan state alone.\n7. `timestamp-lambda-naming` (2, 2), 8. `mypy-weewx-import-overrides` (1, 1),\n9. `coverage-gaps-controller-and-catalogue` (3, 3), 10. `dependency-and-readme-hygiene` (3, 3),\n11. `models-typing-and-validator` (3, 3), 12. `docstring-and-entry-log-trim` (1, 1).\n\n`models-typing-and-validator` is last among the code clusters: its import block in\nmodels/extension_config.py is the same one `defer-to-followup-pr/import-topology` stage 1\nrewrites, and that is the only merge-conflict surface between the two PRs in the whole plan.\n`docstring-and-entry-log-trim` is dead last and deliberately tiny \u2014 it is the volume-over-value\nitem the sense-check guidance warns about, and two of its four target logs are already deleted by\ncluster 1.\n\n## Dependencies\n\nDeclared wherever two clusters open the same file, so nothing is rewritten twice:\n\n- controller.py: `archive-derived-atomic-update` -> init-lifecycle;\n  `controller-callback-typing-and-submit` -> init-lifecycle + archive-derived;\n  `dead-code-and-residue-deletion` -> locale-cache + callback-typing.\n- utils.py: `timestamp-lambda-naming` -> locale-cache + dead-code.\n- pyproject.toml / the publisher pair: `mypy-weewx-import-overrides` -> callback-typing +\n  dead-code + timestamp; `dependency-and-readme-hygiene` -> mypy;\n  `docstring-and-entry-log-trim` -> preprocessor + dead-code.\n- models/extension_config.py: `models-typing-and-validator` -> init-lifecycle.\n- tests/: `coverage-gaps-controller-and-catalogue` -> preprocessor + init-lifecycle + locale-cache,\n  because its one integration test is worth writing only after the three targeted regression tests\n  exist.\n\nThe two dependencies reflect called out are both preserved: the conftest fixture move sits behind\nthe utils cache-reset hook inside cluster 3, and `models-typing-and-validator` is sequenced last.\n\n## Grouping and consolidation\n\nClusters are drawn by file, not by dimension. The widest is\n`dead-code-and-residue-deletion` at four files inside the single directory src/weewx_ha; every\nother cluster is one file plus its tests, or one directory. Reflect's 13 problem-shaped groups\nsurvived the file-proximity redraw unchanged because each was already single-file or\nsingle-directory; no cluster needed splitting.\n\nSteps consolidate per file and per edit, not per finding: 30 steps against 42 issues. The largest\ncollapse is cluster 1, where five findings in a 37-line file become one test step and one edit\nstep, and cluster 2, where five reports of the same half-construction become one test step and\nthree edits. Five clusters were later split one step further to satisfy the validator's\nper-cluster floor, and each split falls on a real seam (source registry vs packaged YAML\ncatalogue; rename vs annotate; the three distinct test targets; the two different README claims;\nper-model-file typing conversions) rather than being padding.\n\n## Rejections carried into the steps\n\nSettled questions are written into the step details so the executor cannot re-litigate them:\ndelete the dead `unit_system` guards rather than harden them (four proofs attached);\n`to_std_system` returns a new dict and is idempotent, so the double conversion is safe and the\nsingle-conversion rework is deferred; the `get_key_config` returns-None claim is REJECTED while\nits zero-coverage test suggestion survives as cluster 9 step 3; `StationInfo.time_zone` stays and\nonly README.md:150 is corrected; weewx must not become a pip runtime dependency; the stale \"use\nHOUR_S\" and `/ 3600.0` clauses in cluster 4 must not be acted on; `functools.cache` and the\ninjected-catalogue redesign are refused in cluster 3; the `convert_lambda` arity change is\nrefused in cluster 7; locale_loader.py:10's coded yaml ignore stays in cluster 8.\n\n## Backlog decisions\n\nExecuted as reflect specified. Only one auto cluster exists in plan state,\n`auto/initial-review`, and it holds zero issue IDs: reflect's decision for it is skip (spent \u2014\nthe twenty blind reviews it schedules have already run and produced these 66 issues), which needs\nno CLI action. The other six names in reflect's backlog section (`auto/dict_keys-phantom_read`,\n`auto/cycles+facade`, `auto/global_mutable_config`, `auto/smells-genuine`,\n`auto/smells-false-positive`, `auto/smells-structural`) are not clusters in plan state, so there\nwas nothing for `plan promote` or `plan skip` to act on: their skip/supersede/promote dispositions\nare discharged by the clustering above \u2014 the smells-genuine items are folded into\n`preprocessor-battery-bitmap` and `controller-callback-typing-and-submit`, the structural ones\ninto the deferred groups, and the deferred local import into `dead-code-and-residue-deletion`.\n\n## Follow-up PR manifest \u2014 now five groups\n\n1. `defer-to-followup-pr/import-topology` (7) \u2014 start with stage 1, `from .. import UnitSystem`\n   -> `from ..utils import UnitSystem` in models/extension_config.py:11.\n2. `defer-to-followup-pr/module-decomposition` (6) \u2014 the archive-DB extraction, the utils.py\n   split, the single-conversion rework, and the deferred Controller test-harness item.\n3. `defer-to-followup-pr/api-naming` (2) \u2014 the three same-named `process_packet` methods.\n4. `defer-to-followup-pr/error-contract` (2, new) \u2014 settle the loader's swallow-versus-raise\n   contradiction in one direction, with the new startup failure mode as the headline.\n\nEvery deferred issue's skip note names its destination group, so the follow-up PR can be opened\nfrom plan state alone.\n",
